    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Even after a full recovery from an initial COVID-19 infection, lingering symptoms arising from a post-viral condition remain a significant concern. Known as post-acute COVID-19 syndrome, or Long COVID, this illness affects every individual differently with its multi-system symptoms containing overwhelming fatigue, brain fog, chest pain, shortness of breath, muscle aches, GI symptoms, nausea, and rashes. According to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), around 10-30% of U.S. adults infected with COVID-19 have had or currently have Long COVID, with an estimated 17 million adults experiencing the condition in some capacity – this number is equivalent to the number of people with cancer (17 million, 2020) in the United States, and nearly as many as those with coronary artery disease (>20 million, 2023), according to the National Institutes of Health (NIH). Given the relatively new onset of this specific medical post-viral syndrome, both patients and healthcare professionals alike are urgently locating opportunities for clinical and translational research to provide answers on the underlying mechanisms behind this condition, as well as accessing more information about the full scale of this complex illness. However, Long COVID currently exists without any definitive biomarkers or curative therapeutics, giving rise to hesitancy and a lack of comprehensive understanding among primary care physicians and the wider healthcare landscape, which impacts Long COVID patients in search of validation for their symptoms and livelihoods that have been dramatically altered. To address this gap in literature, I conducted a mixed-methods study surveying 43 Long COVID patients in the Bay Area, CA and interviewing 5 Long COVID individuals for their narratives on living with Long COVID, accessing qualified physicians, and relying on social-familial support amidst conflicting public narratives on the condition’s scope and basis. Further analysis and thematic coding found that Long COVID patients experience a multitude of symptoms affecting different bodily symptoms for long periods of time, which impacts their financial and occupational situations. Additionally, these patients received high rates of medical pushback and general apathy toward their conditions, leading to hopelessness and instances of doctor hopping (moving around different doctors until one feels adequately understood and cared for). Those with empathetic providers found increased hope and confidence in their resolve and ability to make a recovery over time. Our research reveals current gaps in healthcare and health policy that should be addressed immediately to provide Long COVID patients with better care outcomes and improved quality of life perceptions.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
